// Canonical wait/wake boundary (task-runtime contract "Library and foreign
// providers"; language guide "Waitable Contracts: Retained Substrate
// Direction").
//
// The shared substrate is a small word/value wait plus wake-one/wake-many
// boundary. Libraries build completion waits, locks, barriers, queues, and
// I/O waits over it where the selected target supports that contract. It is
// an engineering direction, not permission to merge unlike host mechanisms
// under false guarantees.
//
// `wait` observes the caller-supplied word at its stable address and releases
// the caller only once a wake edge on that exact word reaches it. A word that
// already differs from `expected` completes without the caller ever waiting:
// the contract compares, it does not park first. The substrate is BLOCKING,
// not suspending: a parked-continuation wait would impose pinning and carry
// evidence on every live borrow at the crossing, which is the task-runtime's
// contract family, not this word substrate's. Suspension, blocking, and
// positive progress stay independent explicit premises; this surface
// publishes blocking and implies no finite wait ceiling. `wake_*` never
// parks.
//
// The word itself is ordinary caller storage taken by shared borrow. The
// substrate owns only the wait/wake edges: protocols that mutate the word
// between edges (release marks, sequence counts, queue heads) establish their
// own atomicity and publication guarantees through the atomic vocabulary.

// Terminal observation of one `WaitWake::wait` call. `Woken` means an exact
// wake edge on the word released the established wait. `Mismatched` means the
// first observation already disagreed with `expected`, so the call completed
// without waiting — the caller's own stale snapshot, never a lost wakeup.
pub data WaitOutcome {
    case Woken;
    case Mismatched;
}

// The one shared substrate capability. `reaches WaitWake` is the reach row
// every provider binds its realization beneath; admission of a concrete
// provider remains a per-target selection, not a consequence of naming this
// contract.
pub boundary trait WaitWake {
    // Release the caller when the wait established on `word` is woken.
    // Completes `Mismatched` immediately if the first observation differs
    // from `expected`. Blocking is the published premise; no finite wait
    // ceiling is implied.
    machine wait(word: &u64, expected: u64) -> WaitOutcome
    reaches WaitWake
    blocks;

    // Release at most one activation waiting on `word`; returns how many
    // wakes the provider actually delivered (0 or 1).
    machine wake_one(word: &u64) -> u64
    reaches WaitWake;

    // Release up to `limit` activations waiting on `word`; returns the count
    // actually released. `limit` 0 is a legal no-wake query.
    machine wake_many(word: &u64, limit: u64) -> u64
    reaches WaitWake;
}
